The alternative method is to assign a name to the previously bound `Pet` py::class_ object and reference it when binding the `Dog` class, by passing the parent `class_` object directly:

```cpp
py::class_<Pet> pet(m, "Pet");
pet.def(py::init<const std::string &>())
    .def_readwrite("name", &Pet::name);

// Method 2: pass parent class_ object:
py::class_<Dog>(m, "Dog", pet /* <- specify Python parent type */)
    .def(py::init<const std::string &>())
    .def("bark", &Dog::bark);
```

This is referred to as "Method 2: pass parent class_ object" in the context, and it is functionality-wise equivalent to Method 1 (using the C++ base class as an extra template parameter).
